WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.900
New enemies, old allies, and a deadly family
feud… the challenges don't look to be any

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.790
easier for John Wick in the fourth chapter
of his revenge story.

00:00:08.378 --> 00:00:12.259
A little over halfway through the first full
trailer for "John Wick: Chapter 4," John Wick

00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:16.180
approaches an outdoor table in Paris behind
which the Eiffel Tower looms large.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.310
There, he sits across from the Marquis de
Gramont in order to discuss the terms of an

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.310
agreement that appears will grant Wick his
freedom from the High Table's pursuit.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:28.310
There's only one condition – he has to defeat
the Marquis in single combat.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:31.990
Briefly visible during this short sequence
are the presences of Winston behind Wick and

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.530
Donnie Yen's Caine on the Marquis' side of
the table.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.290
This configuration offers a look at what seems
to be the two principal factions in "John

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:42.460
Wick: Chapter 4," with Winston notably backing
Wick despite what appeared to be an outright

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.050
betrayal of his old friend at the end of "Chapter
3 – Parabellum."

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:49.920
John Wick is also seen battling Caine at the
trailer's conclusion, suggesting their fight

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.920
might come near the film's climax.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.750
Given Caine's presence at the Marquis' side
earlier on in the trailer, it's likely this

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.359
battle against Caine is directly relevant
to the overarching conflict with the Marquis

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:00.820
with Wick's freedom on the line.

00:01:01.331 --> 00:01:05.530
The final scenes of "John Wick: Chapter 3
– Parabellum" saw the protagonist's long-term

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:08.120
ally, Winston, shoot him over a balcony.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.810
The Continental owner wanted to keep his power,
and he couldn't side with a rogue assassin

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.270
who was being hunted down by all of the High
Table's minions.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:19.440
So, he struck a deal with The Adjudicator
that unfortunately led to John having to take a tumble.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.329
The trailer for "John Wick: Chapter 4" seems
to show John and Winston having a civilized

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:27.670
conversation, suggesting that the former has
forgiven his old ally's transgressions.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.119
At the same time, it's possible that Winston
never intended to kill everyone's favorite

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:33.009
dog-loving assassin in the first place.

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:36.850
After all, Winston was hardly surprised when
The Adjudicator informed him that John had

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.950
survived the aforementioned attack.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.435
Was it part of a plan that only he and John
were in on?

00:01:41.792 --> 00:01:46.140
One particularly interesting aspect of the
"John Wick: Chapter 4" trailer comes at the

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.000
1-minute and 3-second mark when the ever-suave
Winston and the ever-smug Bowery King appear

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.930
to be exiting a subway train.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.741
The two characters have both offered assistance
to John, but they're not exactly known to

00:01:55.741 --> 00:01:56.741
hang out together.

00:01:56.741 --> 00:02:00.350
The implication seems to be that they're together
and that there might even be some sort of

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.200
alliance between the two.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.689
However, the short glimpse only shows the
Bowery King exiting, leaving the actual nature

00:02:05.689 --> 00:02:07.710
of the situation deliciously unclear.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:12.040
Are the two on their way to have a stealthy
meeting, perhaps summoned by John?

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.510
Could Winston be tailing the Bowery King for
some reason?

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:19.060
Are both characters secretly super into crotcheting,
and this sort of stealthy transportation is

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.920
the only way they can meet and make colorful
hacky sacks without losing face in the hardcore

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.154
world they live in?

00:02:24.154 --> 00:02:28.410
It's probably not that last one, but until
further trailers or the movie sheds light

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.769
on their travels' purpose, there's always
hope.

00:02:31.345 --> 00:02:35.410
The High Table in the "John Wick" franchise
is the mysterious organization that essentially

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:38.750
runs the secretive world of assassins and
organized crime.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:42.270
Although each movie reveals another aspect
of this hidden world, the High Table is an

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.770
incredibly powerful organization, and their
word is essentially law.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:50.080
"John Wick: Chapter 3 – Parabellum" features
Wick attempting to clear his name after executing

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.940
a member of the High Table on neutral ground,
which is a grievous mistake in the eyes of

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.400
this organization.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.239
For his actions, Wick is considered incommunicado,
and a large bounty is placed on his head.

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:04.209
After a bombastic finale in "Parabellum,"
Wick escapes from the clutches of the High

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:07.840
Table, and it seems as if he will still have
to deal with them in "John Wick: Chapter 4."

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.910
However, the trailer reveals that his family
sits at the High Table.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:12.700
"I don't sit at the table."

00:03:13.048 --> 00:03:14.366
"Your family does."

00:03:14.366 --> 00:03:18.570
This could mean that he has a sibling or parent
that wields this powerful authority, or it

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.470
could be a reference to the Belarusians from
"Parabellum."

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.989
This may be the reason why John has been able
to survive against such insurmountable odds

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:26.489
time and time again.

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:30.709
Either way, it seems like yet another dynamic
has been interjected into the "John Wick"

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:34.040
mythos, and it will be interesting to see
if this new Wick family individual or group

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.216
on the High Table is a friend or foe.

00:03:36.688 --> 00:03:40.620
While the "John Wick: Chapter 4" trailer is
filled with moments of chaos, intrigue, and

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.220
nail-biting thrills, it does feature a cute
nod to one of Keanu Reeves's most iconic performances

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.510
to break the tension.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:49.460
A minute into the trailer, a freshly suited-up
Wick says,

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:51.500
"I'm going to need a gun."

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.799
Reeves and Fishburne were first seen together
in 1999's "The Matrix."

00:03:55.799 --> 00:03:58.264
In the film, Reeves' character asks for:

00:03:58.264 --> 00:03:59.020
"Guns.

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:01.870
Lots of guns."

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:06.040
The quick line of dialogue from Reeves' Neo
has gone on to become synonymous with "The

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:10.340
Matrix," a film that, in part, is responsible
for the "John Wick" franchise.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:14.920
The late '90s sci-fi flick saw Chad Stahelski
taking on the role of Reeves' stunt double.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.940
Stahelski would later go on to become the
martial arts stunt coordinator for the two

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.130
back-to-back "Matrix" sequels.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:24.229
After a decade-plus of stunt work, Stahelski
decided his next challenge would be directing,

00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:28.270
roping in Reeves to headline the titular role
in 2014's "John Wick."

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:32.110
The second flick in the franchise saw Morpheus
actor Fishburne join the nefarious world of

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:33.480
contract killings.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.997
Now all audiences need is an appearance from
Carrie-Anne Moss to complete the "Matrix" trinity.

00:04:37.997 --> 00:04:41.940
The line from "The Matrix" was previously
referenced verbatim in "John Wick: Chapter

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:42.817
3 – Parabellum."

00:04:43.563 --> 00:04:44.563
"Guns.

00:04:45.206 --> 00:04:46.860
Lots of guns."

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:50.660
Only time will tell just how many times Wick's
fourth outing will reference the Wachowski

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.009
sisters' game-changing sci-fi franchise.

